ad  by fecal oral transmission  also the highest of stars cov2 mortality  and morbidity is in the elderly and in  those with  underlying health problems such as  diabetes  and these groups tend to have less  diverse gut microbiomes  so given these observations further  research into the gi tract  in the context of stars kobe 2 infection  progression  should be considered to gain a better  understanding  of the complexities surrounding this  disease  so now i'm going to discuss the  potential relationship between the gun  microbiome  and the disease severity so  during the initial start cov2 outbreak  in china  early research coming out of the first  affiliated hospital  indicated that bacteria in the gut may  have a significant  impact on the range and the severity of  symptoms associated with sarcoid  infections after profiling the gum  microbiome  of the cytokovich infected individuals  these researchers found  that many of those with more severe  disease exhibited  gut microbiome dysbiosis  specifically there was a decrease in  probiotic organisms including  lactobacillus  and b-fetobacterium and both of these  probiotics are known to have  anti-inflammatory properties  and furthermore these researchers are  suggest  that the use of probiotics may even play  a role  in control of possible secondary  infections  and proliferation of pathogenic  microorganisms  an additional evidence for link between  the gun microbiome and sarcoby ii  comes from a study that reported over 60  percent of individuals with sarcoid  infection displayed gastrointestinal  symptoms  such as diarrhea nausea and vomiting  and this group of subjects also tended  to have  a more serious or critical form of the  disease  based on an analysis of the data the  authors went on to describe  20 proteins associated with disease  severity including  immune factors known to be elevated  during systemic  inflammation they then generated  proteomic risk score  or prs based on these proteins  and using machine learning algorithms  they were able to correlate the prs to  the microbiome  from study subject special samples  demonstrating an association between  microbiome composition and disease  severity  we've taken together these initial  findings  suggest a potential role for the gun  microbiome in the pathogenesis of  cytokine ii infection  finally i'd like to briefly discuss how  the microbiome can potentially  impact vaccine efficacy  surveying efficacy has a number of known  confounding factors such as pre-existing  chronic infections  got associated pathologies use of  immunosuppressive therapies and  antibiotic treatments among others  and over the past decade  various studies have revealed that a  number of these  factors are influenced by the gut  microbiome  so for instance the diversity of the gut  microbiome  plays an important role in the  production of adjuvants  that play key functions in antibody  production  and therefore the potential for  vaccination success against infection  together these observations suggest that  to understand  how our immune systems respond to viral  vaccines and to optimally modulate this  response  we need to study not only the genetics  of the host and the virus in relation to  the vaccine  but also the relationship with the gut  microbiome  and to look at this in a bit more detail  a recent research  demonstrated how gut dysbiosis resulting  from  this the use of antibiotics impacted the  immune response to vaccines  using influenza as an example  but this study showed that a decrease in  microbiome diversity  could be observed up to 180 days after  antibiotic treatment prior to  vaccination  and this dysbiosis led to the  upregulation of inflammation  impaired production of antibodies and  resulted in  an overall dampening of the immune  response  so essentially what's been found is  simple  you need to have healthy immune system  to successfully produce antibodies to  the vaccine  and so you can so you're able to develop  immunity against the virus  and again the diverse and well-balanced  microbiome promotes a healthy  and diverse immune system  okay so to sum up more research  is certainly needed to get a better  understanding  of the association between cyroscov2  infection  and the gut microbiome and the potential  implications of this for optimizing  therapeutic approaches for the disease  as we're all aware there is an urgent  need for effective treatments  based on antivirals and vaccines and  so it's important to explore any  potential  intervention strategies that may exist  and one approach would be by studying  the  gut microbiome profile of individuals  and developing effective dietary or  clinical interventions  to improve gut dysbiosis and thereby  potentially  improving overall immune response in  such  subjects the prebiotics or probiotics  have been  shown to be effective in some studies to  regulate dysbiosis  however there remains insufficient  evidence to support  the blind use of conventional probiotics  as a supplement  so until much more is known about the  pathogenesis of cytokine ii  and its association with the gut  microbiome  scientists suggest that a more targeted  therapeutic approach for  modulation of the gum microbiome is  likely needed  and finally the role of the lung  microbiome  should also be investigated although  technically more  challenging to study insights from  such studies may well add a new  dimension  to the understanding of infectious  diseases  so to conclude in the fight against  infections like coronavirus  a healthy gut microbiome is potentially  a key  factor in maintaining an optimal immune  system  which is especially important given that  both  an over-reactive and under-reactive  immune response can lead to adverse  effects  that could result in clinical  complications  so earlier this year we launched our new  ion mpc microbiome health research kit  we worked with our key research  collaborators to develop  our commensal gut bacterial species  panel  that are associated with immune related  disorders  as an example i want to briefly show you  a pilot study with one of the  researchers  to demonstrate how we can study gut  microbiome  as it relates to disease such as  cytoscopy ii infection  so the data that i'm presenting here is  from a researcher  studying diabetes at a regional research  institute  that is affiliated with a local hospital  so that they have access to  samples from individuals with diabetes  they aim to investigate the gut  microbiome composition in subjects with  different types of diabetes as well as  healthy controls to better understand  the role of the gut microbiome in  diabetes  so in this slide i'm showing an example  data of type 2  diabetes when the authors  compared samples from healthy  individuals to subjects with type 2  diabetes  they saw a clear trend of increased  levels of bacteria  to formicate ratio and  while these these are from the small  sample numbers  the result is consistent with what's  been published in the literature  and at the species level an increased  level of bacteria's vulgaris and  decreased  level of fecalitarian proximity in  subjects with type 2 diabetes were  observed  which is again consistent with the  literature  so finally with high accuracy and  reproducibility that this essay provides  researchers are planning to investigate  the microbiome profile changes  over a time course to study the impact  of clinical or dietary interventions  as well  so one of the key features of this essay  is that  it's a fully validated sample to answer  workflow with an analysis solution also  included  the ion reporter analysis solution for  this essay provides  visualization options for single and  multi-sample analyses  including interactive full taxonomy  classification plot  tech bar plots for relative abundance  heat map and pcoa plots for unsupervised  clustering analysis  and link to chime 2 software  it provides alpha and beta